            <ArticleTitle>Comparison of air and ground transport of cardiac patients.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>33-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To investigate the outcome of cardiac patients transported by helicopter versus ground ambulance</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">A hospital-based helicopter program in southeastern Minnesota</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Retrospective chart review assessing an 18-month period (January 1998 to June 1999). Charts were reviewed for type of cardiac diagnosis, level of pain, treatments en route, time to intervention, and length of stay (LOS). Two-hundred-sixty-six cardiac patients came by helicopter. Of the 86 turndowns, 50 came by ground ambulance; 28 records were recovered in this group. These patients composed the comparison ground group.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Prehospital time was less for patients transported by air than ground transports (P &lt;.001). The amount of time from the call for transport until arrival at our hospital was less for helicopter transports (P =.002). Air transports had more patients with reduced chest pain on arrival. Difference in CCU LOS was not significant (P =.94). Air patients spent an average of 2 fewer days in the hospital than did ground patients (P =.036).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Helicopter transport benefits the cardiac patient with decreased chest pain as a result of more treatments en route; decreased time from the call until arrival, resulting in decreased time to intervention; and shorter prehospital time and hospital stays.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">All of these improved variables relate to salvaged cardiac muscle.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
